COMMAND NAME: groupSession

Starts an interactive command-line session with multiple hosts 
specified in a host file. Will also perform SSH key exchanges 
as the current user (or the user specified in the host file).

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

groupSession <host_file> [-p <ssh_port>] [-s] [-v] [-h]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The groupSession utility starts an interactive command-line 
session that allows you to run bash shell commands on multiple 
hosts at the same time. The groupSession utility uses a host list 
file to determine the hosts (and optionally the users) involved in 
a session. You cannot change the host list file mid-session. If you 
want to change the host list file, you must start a new groupSession 
instance after editing the file. 

The groupSession utility will also exchange SSH keys between the hosts 
at the start of a session if you do not have a trusted host environment 
established already. This allows SSH connections to the remote hosts 
without a password prompt. If a user name is not specified in the host 
list file, then the current user will be used. 

Greenplum recommends performing the key exchange process twice: 
once as root and once as the mppadmin user (the user designated to own 
your Bizgres MPP installation). The Bizgres MPP management scripts require 
that the same non-root user be created on all hosts in the Bizgres MPP 
system, and the scripts must be able to connect as that user to all 
hosts without a password prompt.

When exchanging SSH keys, hit the ENTER key when prompted for the 
ssh-agent pass key phrase (do NOT enter a pass key phrase). 
For example:

groupSession: Remote password for host kite6: <user_password>
groupSession: ssh-agent pass key phrase: [ENTER]

Once your trusted host environment is established, you get the 
groupSession command prompt (>). You can enter a command as you 
would in a regular bash terminal command-line, and the command will be 
executed on all hosts in the host list file. To run a command on the 
local host only, prefix the command with ! or shell. Use rcopy to copy 
a file to a specified location on all hosts.

*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

<host_file>

Required. The name of a file that contains a list of hosts that 
will participate in the group session. The groupSession utility will 
also exchange SSH keys as either the current user or the username 
specified in the host file. The format of the host file is as follows:

username@host1
username@host2
...
username@hostN

OR (if you want to connect as the current user)

host1
host2
...
hostN



-p <ssh_port>

Optional. If you are using a different SSH port besides the default 
port of 22, use this option to specify the SSH port number.

-s

Optional. Disable the setup of trusted host key exchange. The 
default behavior is to exchange SSH keys as the specified user 
between the hosts in the host list.

-v

Optional. Verbose mode will report all commands sent to a 
host and the output received.

-h

Displays the online help.


*****************************************************
COMMANDS (AT THE groupSession COMMAND PROMPT)
*****************************************************

At the groupSession command prompt (>), you can enter a command 
as you would in a regular bash terminal command-line and the 
command will be executed on all hosts in the host list file. To run
a command on the local host only, prefix the command with ! or shell. 
Use the rcopy command to copy a file to a specified location on all hosts.

<command>
Enter any command as you would at a regular bash terminal command-line. This 
will execute the given command on all of the hosts in the host list file.


shell <command>
Commands prefixed with shell will run the command on the local host only.


! <command>
Commands prefixed with ! will run the command on the local host only.


rcopy [<filename> [<path_on_remote_hosts>]]
The rcopy command is used to copy the specified file to the specified 
directory location on all of the hosts in the host list file.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Start groupSession and exchange SSH keys as the current user with 
the hosts listed in host_file:

./groupSession host_file -v

groupSession: Remote password for host kite6: <user_password>

groupSession: ssh-agent pass key phrase: [ENTER]

groupSession: Remote password for host kite7: <user_password>

groupSession: ssh-agent pass key phrase: [ENTER]

Keys for mppadmin on kite 7 have been installed.

Keys for mppadmin on kite 6 have been installed.

Acquired passwords, keys.

Entering command prompt, shh commands are executed on all hosts:

enter command (q to quit, rcopy to copy file)>



At the groupSession command prompt, copy a file to the specified
location on all the hosts in host_file.

rcopy mpp-2.0.tar.gz /



At the groupSession command prompt, run a shell command on all 
the hosts in host_file.

ls -a /mppdata/dbfast1



At the groupSession command prompt, run a shell command on the 
local host only.

shell cat /etc/profile

OR

! cat /etc/profile

